<Layout id="branchprediction" title="Branch Prediction">

  <h1>Branch Prediction</h1>


  <h2>Branch Prediction in CMS through dynamic profiling</h2>

  <p>
    The CMS isn't just a 1 to 1 intepreter but instead monitors the behavior of the x86 code.
    Over time, the CMS collects runtime information which includes the frequency of a branch being taken.
    It uses this branch information (along with other metrics) to create a native translation block which by default, assumes
    the branch being taken or not taken. Public documentation does not specify the detailed algorithm behind it.
  </p>

   <img
    src="/images/Prediction.png"
    alt="Transmeta Crusoe pipeline showing the ALU, load/store, floating-point, and branch pipelines"
    class="figure"
  />

<p>
    The CMS goes further and builds these optimized native translation blocks for entire common pathways for code.
    This is why in bechmarks, the Crusoe performs better after running a program once.
  </p>


<h2>Clever optimizations</h2>

  <p>
    Although not the same as branch prediction, CMS can also do clever optimizations such as removing a branch when
    both sides of the branch are cheap to compute. 
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <pre class="code-example"><code>if (condition)
    y = A;
else
    y = B;</code></pre>

  <p>
    Both sides of the condition are computed, which allows the CMS to just select the correct
    value later. This also gives the CMS more oppertunities to fill the molecules, avoiding NOPs.
  </p>


  <h2>Branching hardware</h2>

  <p>Since all the prediction is done though software, the branch unit in the pipeline is
  used to verify that the prediction was indeed correct.</p>



</Layout>